  "articleBody": "Starting a custom molding project can be exciting, but there are some common slip-ups that can really set you back. One of the biggest mistakes is jumping in without a clear part specification. You need to know exactly what you want and communicate it thoroughly. Another frequent issue is not sharing enough design data; the more information your molder has, the better they can create what you envision. Underestimating your volume needs is another trap—this can lead to tooling choices that aren't right for your long-term production. And please, don't skip the prototyping or DFM (Design for Manufacturability) review! These steps are critical for catching issues early and avoiding expensive rework down the line. To avoid these headaches, make sure you're clear on your requirements, share all relevant design information, and get an accurate forecast of your production volumes. Working with a molder who offers in-house tooling and design support can be a huge advantage. They can guide you through the process and help you make smart decisions from the get-go, especially when precision is key.",
  "datePublished": "2026-08-09"
}